    "author_association": "NONE",
    "body": "### Problem\n\n## Description\nI have been using the builtin package manager and I *love* it! Thanks so much! I think it might be beneficial to have some sort of interface for the builtin package manager from the command line for automated system tools to update packages and clean up inactive packages. It is possible you already have plans for maybe a better package interface or something but I thought I would just put this out here to show how I use it to update packages.\n\n### Current State\nI currently have a script that updates all the things on my system that require it, and for neovim I use:\n```bash\nnvim --headless -c \":lua vim.pack.update(nil, { force = true }); os.exit(0)\"\n#=> vim.pack: Updating (0/4)\n#=> vim.pack:  25% Updating (1/4) - plenary.nvim\n#=> vim.pack:  50% Updating (2/4) - vim-svelte-plugin\n#=> vim.pack:  75% Updating (3/4) - conform.nvim\n#=> vim.pack: 100% Updating (4/4) - telescope.nvim\n```\n\n### Expected behavior\n\nIt might be nice to have a simple way to do this for tooling as well. *Ideally* a subcommand would be great, however I know nvim does not currently have any subcommands so I imagine adding one would be a headache to add one. \n\n```bash\n# Update all packages\nnvim --pack-update\n# Clean up or delete packages that are no longer active\n# Nice to have instead of running `:lua vim.pack.update()` and `gra` motion to remove the package.\nnvim --pack-clean\n```",
    "closed_at": "2026-09-18T16:39:51Z",
